ndamental peripheral mechanism in migraine initiation. This sensitization leads to the release of calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) and other inflammatory mediators, contributing to neurogenic inflammation and central sensitization. [Data: Entities (425, 419); Relationships (544)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Trigeminal nerve as peripheral initiator of migraine\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The central nervous system (CNS), encompassing the brain and spinal cord, is the overarching framework in which all migraine-related neural structures operate. Evidence suggests that migraine attacks originate within CNS structures such as the hypothalamus and brainstem, with subsequent propagation through the thalamus and cortex. The CNS integrates sensory, autonomic, and cognitive inputs, and its dysregulation is central to the pathophysiology of migraine. The involvement of multiple CNS regions highlights the complexity of migraine as a disorder of central network dysfunction rather than a simple peripheral pain condition. [Data: Entities (426, 420, 421, 422, 423); Relationships (547, 551, 552, 548, 545)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Central nervous system as the integrative framework for migraine\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The trigeminal vascular system is a specialized neurovascular complex that integrates peripheral sensory input with vascular responses in the meninges. It is activated during migraine attacks and is responsible for transmitting nociceptive signals from the periphery to the brainstem. This system includes the trigeminal nerve's peripheral terminals, meningeal blood vessels, and associated glial and immune cells. Its activation leads to the release of vasoactive peptides such as CGRP, which contribute to vasodilation and neurogenic inflammation, further amplifying pain signaling. [Data: Entities (419, 425); Relationships (544, 540)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Trigeminal vascular system as a